\f0\fs22 \cf2 Now, four years on, the tin was open on the table and her mother was not doing any of it in the right order.\
She did the injection first, which she had never once done, and she did not do the arm measurements at all, and she did not get the pencil, and when Sarah looked at her, her mother was already going back into the tin.\
\u8220 ?You did it,\u8221 ? Sarah said.\
\u8220 ?I know.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?You already did it.\u8221 ?\
Her mother took out the rest of them. There were five, and Sarah had never seen more than one out of the tin at a time, and had not known until that moment that there was ever more than one in there.\
\u8220 ?Hold your arm.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Why?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Hold your arm out, Sarah.\u8221 ?\
She held her arm out, because she was eleven, and because her mother had her coat on, and because the last four days her mother had not come home until Sarah was asleep and the two days before that she had not come home at all, and because whatever was happening in the kitchen was very obviously not about the yellow form and Sarah could not work out what else there was for it to be about.\
Her mother did all five. She was quick and she was not gentle and she did not talk, and the fourth one went in badly and left a bruise that would be gone by Friday.\
Then she put the empty tin in her bag, and did not put the bowl away, and did not take the pencil out at all.\
There was no spoon of anything afterwards. There had been a spoon of something afterwards since Sarah was seven.\
Sarah pulled her sleeve down. Her arm was hot from the inside, in five places.\
\u8220 ?Am I in trouble?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?No.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?It\u8217 ?s because of the form.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?It\u8217 ?s not because of the form.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Then why are you \u8212 ?\u8221 ?\
Her mother crouched down. She did not do that either. She put both hands on the sides of Sarah\u8217 ?s head, one over each ear, which she had done when Sarah was small and had not done for years, and held her there for a moment, and Sarah could feel the cold of the outside still on her mother\u8217 ?s fingers.\
\u8220 ?I\u8217 ?ll be back for your birthday,\u8221 ? her mother said.\
Then she stood up and picked up her bag and went to work.\
She had left grey dust on the kitchen floor where she crouched, and Sarah, who had eleven days of grievance in her and nowhere to put it, got the dustpan out and swept it up so that it would be gone before morning, and did not think about it again for a very long time.\
The door went at eighteen minutes to nine.\
Sarah went and had a look at the form, which was still on the table, still with the heavy line ruled across the middle of it, and then she went to bed.\
\
The cake was bought early, on the Thursday, because Sarah\u8217 ?s aunt believed in getting in ahead of a crowd.\
By the Saturday there was no reason to buy another one, so they had that one, and it was fine. Sarah had a piece. Her aunt had two and said something about her figure that she plainly did not mean.\
Nobody had rung. Nobody rang the following week either, or the week after that, and there was no letter, and when the aunt finally got somebody at the number on the payslip, the woman on the other end said the department did not exist and had not existed, and then said it again more slowly, as though the fault were in the hearing.\
Sarah went back to school on the Monday. She had not handed the yellow form in and the office would not refund the two dollars fifty because the closing date had passed.\
Her aunt, on the phone, on the subject of the man in the office, said men ain\u8217 ?t nothin\u8217 ? but dicks and hormones, which Sarah wrote down in the back of an exercise book because it was the funniest thing she had ever heard, and which she was still saying two centuries later, at parties, to people who had no idea she was quoting anyone.}